"""Adult life essentials, unit 1: how to live a full life without being used up by it."""
from spec import L, U

A = U('Living well when life is full', 'Morality', 'How do I keep my time, my attention and my money from running my life?', [
L('Rest is a commandment, not a reward',
  'We treat rest as something we earn once everything is done, so it never comes. Scripture does the opposite: God rests at the end of creation, Israel is freed from Egypt to rest, and the Church keeps Sunday as the day work is not allowed to be the last word. Rest is a way of saying, “I am not what I produce, and the world does not depend on me.”',
  '345-349,2168-2173,2184-2188,2427-2428,2094',
  'Genesis 2:2-3;Exodus 20:8-11;Deuteronomy 5:12-15;Mark 2:23-28',
  'sabbath sunday prayer',
  'In the last month, when did you actually rest, and what made it possible or impossible?',
  'Draw the last seven days as a simple grid. Colour what was work, what was rest, what was neither (scrolling, errands, worrying). Ask what a real Sabbath would have to protect, and choose one four-hour block next Sunday to guard like an appointment.',
  'serenity',
  id='YA-L1',
  hook='Ask everyone to hold up a hand showing how many hours of the last week they would honestly call rest. Do not comment. Then read Exodus 20:8–11 and Deuteronomy 5:12–15 side by side and ask: why do the two versions give different reasons?',
  teach=[
    'Two reasons for the Sabbath, not one. Exodus grounds it in creation: God rested, and “creation was fashioned with a view to the sabbath” (CCC 345–348). Deuteronomy grounds it in freedom: you were a slave in Egypt, and slaves never get a day off. Rest is therefore both a gift of being a creature and a sign of being free (CCC 2168–2172).',
    'The Church moves the day to Sunday because it is the day of the Resurrection, “the first day of the week” and “the eighth day,” the beginning of the new creation (CCC 2174–2176). Sunday Mass is not an add-on to the rest; it is what the rest is for. We stop working so that we can receive something, not only so that we can recover (CCC 2184–2185).',
    'The Catechism asks for more than attendance: “Just as God ‘rested on the seventh day from all his work,’ human life has a rhythm of work and rest” (CCC 2184). Works of mercy, family time, and “leisure for the mind and body” belong to the day, and it warns against making others work because we cannot stop (CCC 2186–2187).',
    'This links to a lesson from the first commandment. Among the sins against God the Catechism lists acedia, the lazy or joyless neglect of the good (CCC 2094; 2733). Restlessness and sloth are two faces of the same thing: neither can rest, and neither can really work. Work, too, is dignified when it serves the person and does not swallow him (CCC 2427–2428).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between resting and numbing out? How can you tell afterward?',
    'Who is made to work so that you can have your Sunday (shops, restaurants, deliveries)? What, if anything, should you do about it?',
    'What would it look like to trust that the world will not fall apart if you stop for a day?',
    'Is Sunday a burden or a gift in your family? Why?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Why does the Church treat rest as a matter of faith and not just health?|It is only a health recommendation|It is a way of saying that we are creatures who do not hold the world together, and that we are free|It was invented to reduce unemployment|It applies only to Jews|1|CCC 345–348 and 2184–2185: rest imitates God and proclaims freedom and trust.',
    'Why did the Church move the day of worship to Sunday?|It was more convenient|Sunday is the day of the Resurrection, the first day of the new creation|The Roman Empire required it|Saturday was abolished by law|1|CCC 2174–2176.',
    'Which of these is closest to the Sabbath the Church describes?|A day for catching up on work|A day of Mass, rest, family and mercy, protected on purpose|A day of strict silence|Any free evening|1|CCC 2184–2188: worship, rest, and works of mercy.',
  ],
  life='Choose one stretch of Sunday, four hours or more, and protect it. Decide in advance what is off limits (email, errands, chores, shopping) and what is in (Mass, a meal, a walk, a visit). Tell one person so they can hold you to it. Report back honestly next week.',
  saint='Josemaría Escrivá: a priest who taught that ordinary work done for God becomes prayer, and also insisted that rest and prayer must not be squeezed out.',
  misc=[
    '“Rest is laziness.”',
    '“Sunday observance is only about getting to Mass.”',
    '“If I rest, I will fall behind, and then I will deserve to rest.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:sunday-sabbath', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.” (Matthew 11:28)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain in two sentences why rest is a theological and not only a practical matter, and to name the one protected block of time they have chosen.',
  script=dict(
    open='Say, “I am going to give you thirty seconds of silence. Do nothing. No phones, no fidgeting.” Time it. Then ask, “How did that feel?” Expect honest discomfort. Say, “That discomfort is the topic tonight.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “We usually treat rest as a reward. You finish the list, then you rest. But the list never ends, so the rest never comes. The Bible does it the other way around. Look at Genesis 1: God works six days and the seventh is the crown of creation. Rest is not an afterthought. It is the point.”',
      'Say: “Two versions of the commandment. Exodus 20 says: rest because God rested. Deuteronomy 5 says: rest because you were slaves in Egypt, and the Lord brought you out. Put them together: you rest because you are a creature, not the Creator, and because you are free. A slave cannot take a day off. If you cannot stop, ask whom you are really serving.”',
      'Say: “The Church moves the day to Sunday because Christ rose on the first day. Sunday is both the first day and the eighth day: the day the new creation began. So Sunday rest is not just recovery. We stop in order to receive: Mass, one another, God. The Catechism, number 2184, calls it a day of joy and of rest from work. It also asks us not to make others work so we do not have to stop.”',
      'Say: “One more thing. The Catechism lists acedia, spiritual sloth, as a sin against God (2094). Notice that it is not a quiet vice. A person who cannot rest and a person who cannot be bothered are often the same person: they are both avoiding the question of what they are for. Rest well and work well come from the same trust.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What fills the hours you would call rest, and which of them truly restore you? (Listen for numbing versus restoring.)',
      'Who works so you can have your Sunday? What might we do about it? (Small acts: shop less on Sunday, tip, thank, pray for them.)',
      'What are you afraid would happen if you stopped for a full day? (Name the fear; it is usually about worth.)',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give everyone the grid of last week’s seven days. Colour work, rest, and “neither.” Do this in silence for five minutes.',
      'In pairs, each person names the block they will guard next Sunday and what is in and out of it.',
      'Each person writes one sentence: “On Sunday I will stop in order to ____.” Keep it.',
    ],
    close='Pray the serenity prayer together slowly. Then say, “Rest is a work of trust. Practice it this week and tell me how it went.”'),
  tryit=['Colour the grid of your last seven days: work, rest, neither.', 'Pick one four-hour block of Sunday and say what is in and out.', 'Tell a partner, so they can ask you next week.'],
  saintStory='Josemaría Escrivá was born in Spain in 1902. As a young priest he began to see something many people missed: that ordinary work, done well and offered to God, is a way to holiness and not a hindrance to it. In 1928 he founded Opus Dei to spread that idea to lawyers, nurses, farmers and mothers, and not only to monks.\nHe was also a realist about people. He wrote that a tired mind cannot pray, that a person who never stops is often hiding from something, and that rest, family life and good humour are part of a holy life. He told his followers to take rest as seriously as work, and to make it an act of trust.\nHe died in 1975 and was canonized in 2002 by John Paul II, who called him the saint of ordinary life.',
),

L('Attention: what you look at becomes what you love',
  'Most of the battle for a good life is fought over what we pay attention to, not over big decisions. The Catechism’s teaching on purity of heart, on desire, and on distraction in prayer all say the same thing: the heart goes where the eyes and the mind keep going. Freedom is the slow work of choosing what gets your attention.',
  '2514-2527,2534-2540,2729-2733,1865-1866,2015',
  'Matthew 6:19-24;Luke 10:38-42;Philippians 4:8',
  'virtue prayer temperance chastity',
  'What did you give your attention to yesterday that you would not choose to give it to again?',
  'For three days keep a small tally in the margin of your notes: each time you reached for your phone without a reason, mark it, and mark what you felt just before. Bring the tally and decide one change that costs you something.',
  'come-holy-spirit',
  id='YA-L2',
  hook='Ask the group to put their phones on the table, face up, and not touch them for the lesson. Then ask: “What did you feel in the first two minutes?” Then read Luke 10:41–42: “Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things.”',
  teach=[
    'Purity of heart is not an extra for the very pious. The Beatitude, “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God” (Matthew 5:8), is about the whole person: what we want, what we see, what we think. “The pure of heart” are those who have attuned their intellect and will to the demands of God’s holiness (CCC 2518). Notice that the promise is to see God. Attention is how we see.',
    'The Catechism connects purity to a battle of desire: “The heart is the seat of moral personality: from within come evil designs” (CCC 2517). The battle is fought by baptismal grace, by chastity, by modesty, by purity of intention and of vision, and by prayer (CCC 2520–2527). Modesty is not a dress code; it “protects the mystery of persons and their love” and invites patience and moderation in relationships (CCC 2521–2522).',
    'The tenth commandment goes deeper: it forbids greed, envy, and the endless wanting that turns every good thing into a comparison. The remedy is not stern self-denial but “poverty of heart” and the desire to see God (CCC 2535–2540; 2544–2548). If our wanting is trained on God, the other desires fall into order.',
    'Prayer exposes the problem fastest. The Catechism treats distraction as the most habitual difficulty in prayer and says it reveals “what we are attached to” (CCC 2729). Acedia, the loss of heart for the good, is a form of “depression due to lax ascetical practice” (CCC 2733). The cure is not willpower alone but returning, again and again, to the one thing: “Mary has chosen the good portion” (Luke 10:42).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What does your phone, feed, or screen teach you to want?',
    'Where do you feel envy most easily, and what is it telling you that you really want?',
    'Why would the Church call attention a moral matter and not just a productivity matter?',
    'What small act of attention (a meal without a screen, a walk without headphones) would change your week?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Beatitude “Blessed are the pure in heart” promise?|A life without temptation|That they will see God|Immediate wealth|Praise from others|1|Matthew 5:8 and CCC 2518–2519.',
    'According to the Catechism, what does distraction in prayer reveal?|That prayer is pointless|What we are attached to|That we have no faith|That God is far away|1|CCC 2729: the heart is revealed in what we keep returning to.',
    'What is the Church’s remedy for envy and endless wanting?|Pretend not to want things|Poverty of heart and a desire to see God|Buy less and complain more|Avoid all people who have more|1|CCC 2544–2548: the desire for God orders the other desires.',
  ],
  life='For one week, pick a daily “fast of attention”: no phone at meals, or the first twenty minutes of the day screen-free, or one walk without headphones. Spend the freed time on one thing you love: a person, prayer, a book. At the end of the week write three sentences about what changed.',
  saint='Blaise Pascal: a mathematician and inventor who wrote that all of humanity’s troubles come from not being able to sit quietly in a room alone, and who spent his last years in prayer.',
  misc=[
    '“Purity is only about sex.”',
    '“Distraction in prayer means the prayer did not count.”',
    '“Wanting things is a sin.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:chastity', 'room:teresa-castle'],
  mem='“Whatever is true, whatever is honorable, whatever is just, whatever is pure, whatever is lovely, think about these things.” (Philippians 4:8)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain how attention is a moral question, using one idea from purity of heart and one from prayer.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask everyone to put their phones face up on the table. Say, “We are going to do the whole lesson with them there. Do not touch them.” After two minutes, ask, “What did that feel like?” Say, “That pull is what we are talking about.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Most of us think of morality as big choices. But most of our moral life is decided by what we look at for ten seconds at a time. Jesus says, ‘Where your treasure is, there will your heart be also’ (Matthew 6:21). Treasure here is whatever you keep returning to.”',
      'Say: “The Beatitude is: ‘Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.’ That is not mainly about sex. The Catechism (2518) says purity of heart means our mind and will are in tune with God’s holiness, in three areas: charity, chastity, and love of truth and orthodoxy. It is a freedom to see and love rightly. Seeing is the whole idea. Attention is how we see.”',
      'Say: “The tenth commandment is about wanting. Envy and greed turn every good thing into a comparison. The Church’s answer is not to want nothing, but to want God first. The Catechism (2544) calls it poverty of heart. If the deep desire is God, then smaller desires find their place.”',
      'Say: “Prayer is where we feel it. Distraction in prayer, says the Catechism (2729), shows what we are attached to. And acedia, spiritual laziness, is described as a depression from lax spiritual habits (2733). So the fix is small and daily: come back. You will drift. Come back. That is not failure; that is the practice.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do you reach for when you are bored, anxious or lonely? What is it giving you, and what is it taking?',
      'Where do you feel envy, and what does it tell you that you really want?',
      'What is one place you could put a boundary around your attention, and what would you do with the time?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give everyone a card. Write on one side: “What I want most.” On the other: “What I give most of my attention to.” Ask them to compare in silence.',
      'In pairs, share one thing from the card you are willing to change this week, and one reason.',
      'Name your attention fast for the week and write it on the card. Keep the card in your wallet or case.',
    ],
    close='Pray the Come, Holy Spirit together slowly. Say, “Attention is love in motion. Spend it on purpose this week.”'),
  tryit=['Compare what you want most with what you attend to most.', 'Choose one boundary for your attention this week.', 'Write it on a card and keep it where you will see it.'],
  saintStory='Blaise Pascal was born in France in 1623. By sixteen he had written an important paper on geometry; by his twenties he had built one of the first mechanical calculators and shown how air pressure works. People thought he was the finest mind of his day.\nIn 1654 something happened that he never described fully. On the night of November 23 he had an experience of God so intense that he sewed a note about it into the lining of his coat and carried it until he died. It begins, “Fire. God of Abraham, God of Isaac, God of Jacob, not of the philosophers and scholars.” After that he gave his life to prayer, to the poor, and to writing what became the Pensées.\nIn it he wrote the line that has not aged: “All of humanity’s problems stem from man’s inability to sit quietly in a room alone.” He died in 1662, aged 39.',
),

L('Money: enough, and what the rest is for',
  'Most people never ask what “enough” is, so no amount is. The Church holds two truths at once: private property is good and necessary, and all goods are first given for everyone. Between those two sits a life of working honestly, spending wisely, and giving until it costs something. Wealth is not the sin; being owned by it is.',
  '2402-2406,2443-2449,2544-2547,2461,1938,2429',
  'Luke 12:13-34;Luke 16:19-31;2 Corinthians 8:1-15;Matthew 6:19-21',
  'stewardship almsgiving works-of-mercy justice',
  'If someone read your bank statements, what would they say you love most?',
  'List your last month’s spending in four buckets: necessary, comfortable, wanted, given away. Decide how much of the third belongs in the fourth, and set up one recurring gift this week.',
  'st-francis',
  id='YA-L3',
  hook='Ask: “How much money would be enough for you? Write the number down but do not show anyone.” Ask them to look at it. Then ask how it has changed over their life. Read Luke 12:15: “Take heed, and beware of all covetousness.”',
  teach=[
    'Two principles that must be held together. First: private property is legitimate and good; it protects freedom and the family (CCC 2403). Second: “the universal destination of goods” — God gave the earth to all, so that ownership is always held for the common good (CCC 2402–2406). Neither the miser nor the thief has the whole truth.',
    'Almsgiving is a matter of justice as well as charity. St. John Chrysostom, quoted in the Catechism: “Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them. The goods we possess are not ours, but theirs” (CCC 2446). That is a startling sentence, and the Church means it. It is why the works of mercy are a duty, not a bonus (CCC 2447).',
    'Why is the love of money so dangerous? Because “the love of riches is incompatible with love of the poor” in practice (CCC 2461), and because desire for money grows by feeding (CCC 2536; 2547). Jesus’ first Beatitude is “Blessed are the poor in spirit.” That is not a call to be penniless; it is detachment, which lets us use what we have freely (CCC 2544–2547).',
    'Work matters too. Honest work, fair wages and faithful stewardship are part of the seventh commandment (CCC 2427–2434; 2409–2414). A generous person is also a person who pays what is owed, keeps promises and does not waste. Generosity that ignores justice is vanity.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'When have you felt owned by money, or by the lack of it?',
    'What does “enough” look like for you, honestly?',
    'Is giving until it costs something possible in your situation? What would that look like?',
    'How do you hold together paying what is owed and giving freely?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the “universal destination of goods” mean?|Everyone must own the same amount|God gave the earth to all, so ownership is held with the common good in view|Private property is wrong|Governments own everything|1|CCC 2402–2406.',
    'What does Chrysostom, quoted in CCC 2446, say about refusing to share with the poor?|It is a private matter|It is a kind of stealing, because those goods are theirs too|It is only wrong if you are rich|It is allowed if you give to the Church|1|CCC 2446.',
    'What does “poor in spirit” mean?|Having no possessions|Being unable to afford things|Being detached from wealth and dependent on God|Being shy|2|CCC 2544–2547: detachment and trust.',
  ],
  life='This week, list your spending for the last month in four buckets: needs, comforts, wants, giving. Decide on one change that makes the fourth bucket larger by a sum that you will feel. Set it up as an automatic gift, to a parish, a poor family you know, or a charity you have checked.',
  saint='Elizabeth Ann Seton: a wealthy New York widow who lost her fortune, became Catholic, and founded the first American Catholic free school.',
  misc=[
    '“The Church wants everyone to be poor.”',
    '“Charity is optional, justice is the government’s job.”',
    '“I will give once I have enough.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:tithing-and-money', 'apol:social-justice-poor'],
  mem='“For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.” (Matthew 6:21)',
  assess='Ask each person to state both principles (private property and the universal destination of goods) and name one concrete gift they will make this month.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask each person to write, privately, the amount of money they think would be enough. Say, “Fold it. We will come back to it.” Then say, “Almost no one picks the number they have now.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Church does not condemn wealth. But Jesus says more about money than about almost anything except the Kingdom. Luke 12: a man asks Jesus to settle an inheritance. Jesus refuses and tells a story about a farmer whose barns are full. God calls him a fool, not because he saved, but because he saved for himself.”',
      'Say: “Two truths. One: private property is good. It protects families and freedom (CCC 2403). Two: God gave the earth to everyone. That is the universal destination of goods (2402). So what I own, I own as a steward. The miser forgets the second. The thief forgets the first.”',
      'Say: “Here is a sentence from Chrysostom in the Catechism, 2446: ‘Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them. The goods we possess are not ours, but theirs.’ That sentence is hard. It does not say you are a thief. It says the poor have a claim on your surplus, and giving is part of justice.”',
      'Say: “Jesus does not say ‘Blessed are the poor.’ He says ‘Blessed are the poor in spirit.’ That is detachment: using money without being used by it. The test is simple: Can you give some away without a fight? If not, money may be holding you. Start small, but start.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Look at your folded number. Is it more than you have now? What does that tell you?',
      'What is one thing you spend on that you do not even enjoy? What could that money do for someone else?',
      'Where would your giving cost you something real? What is stopping you?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give everyone a sheet with four buckets. Fill in a rough month of spending in silence.',
      'In pairs, name one number you will move from comfort or wants to giving.',
      'Write how you will do it (automatic gift, envelope, named person) and when. Take a photo of the plan.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer of St. Francis together. Say, “Give until it is a little uncomfortable. That is where you start to be free.”'),
  tryit=['Sort last month into needs, comforts, wants, giving.', 'Name one amount to move into giving.', 'Choose the recipient and set the date.'],
  saintStory='Elizabeth Ann Bayley was born in New York in 1774 into a wealthy Episcopalian family. She married William Seton, a merchant, and had five children. Then the business failed, and William fell ill with tuberculosis. In 1803 she took him to Italy hoping the climate would help. He died there within weeks, and she was left a widow with five children and no money.\nWhile in Italy she was shown great kindness by a Catholic family, the Filicchis, and she was moved by the Mass and the belief in the Real Presence. Back in America she became Catholic in 1805, and lost most of her friends for it.\nShe opened a small school in Baltimore, and later in Emmitsburg, Maryland, and founded the Sisters of Charity, the first American religious community. She died in 1821 and was canonized in 1975, the first American-born saint.',
),
])
